WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.542
This practice improves the billing CASE by giving an exact zero its own business meaning.

00:00:05.542 --> 00:00:12.357
Without a special branch, zero is simply less than five thousand, so it would fall into Low-Value.

00:00:12.357 --> 00:00:19.992
But in this dataset, a zero bill represents No Charge, which is meaningfully different from a small positive

00:00:19.992 --> 00:00:23.482
charge. The order should be deliberate.

00:00:23.482 --> 00:00:25.087
Handle NULL first as Unknown.

00:00:25.087 --> 00:00:28.227
Then handle exactly zero as No Charge.

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:31.972
After that, apply the high and standard thresholds.

00:00:31.972 --> 00:00:35.532
ELSE can represent the remaining positive low-value bills.

00:00:35.532 --> 00:00:41.662
This is a good example of why domain meaning matters more than pure mathematics.

00:00:41.662 --> 00:00:45.772
A zero-dollar hotel bill may mean complimentary stay.

00:00:45.772 --> 00:00:49.672
A zero inventory count may mean out of stock.

00:00:49.672 --> 00:00:51.912
A zero test score may be a real observed score.

00:00:51.912 --> 00:00:56.587
A NULL test score may mean the student never took the exam.

00:00:56.587 --> 00:01:03.042
Technically, zero and NULL are both “not a positive amount,” but analytically they can mean very different

00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:08.577
things. Run the query and inspect the zero-bill record.

00:01:08.577 --> 00:01:12.237
Make sure it now receives No Charge rather than Low-Value.
